   "text": {
    "L1": "The poem's central idea is simple: when you fail, do not give up. Try again and keep practising.",
    "L2": "A repeated line, such as “Try, try again,” is a refrain. Repetition fixes the message in our mind, like practice itself.",
    "L3": "The kite or climb is an example, but the lesson is for life: keep trying after you fail.",
    "L4": "Listen to the line endings: sore and more rhyme because their sounds match.",
    "L5": "A stanza can use a rhyme scheme. In the seed stanza, ground and sound, then out and sprout, make AABB.",
    "L6": "The first conditional has simple present in the if-part. The result part uses will plus the base verb.",
    "L7": "So we say, “If you practise, you will improve.” We do not put will in the if-part.",
    "L8": "Now choose the sentence that uses the first conditional correctly."
   },
